{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CswAq38WDQyAJTqcRNMGH1","type":"tool_result","content":"8832\ttask: |\n8833\t    Follow these steps in order. Complete each step before moving to the next.\n8834\t\n8835\t    1. **Review provided context** — The project_context section above already contains all project documentation, SRDs, source code, shared_protocols, and package_contracts inline in this prompt. Review Required Reading files by scrolling up to find them in project_context — do NOT call the Read, Glob, or Grep tools for any file already present above. The only files you may read with tools are files NOT in project_context (e.g. files you are about to create or modify).\n8836\t\n8837\t    2. **Plan approach** — Before writing code, determine:\n8838\t       - What exists already (interfaces, types, patterns in the source code above)\n8839\t       - What patterns to follow (from the provided source code and execution_constitution)\n8840\t       - What the acceptance criteria require\n8841\t\n8842\t    3. **Implement** — Complete the work described in Requirements and Files to Create/Modify. Follow the Design Decisions. Do not deviate from the specified files unless a requirement makes it necessary.\n8843\t\n8844\t    4. **Verify** — Check every item in Acceptance Criteria. Run tests if the criteria require it. Do not skip any criterion.\n8845\tconstraints: |\n8846\t    - Do NOT read any file in the repository to infer style, patterns, or conventions. All style guidance is provided in go_style_constitution above. All source patterns are provided in project_context above. If a file you need is absent from project_context, write it from scratch following go_style_constitution — do not read the filesystem to fill the gap.\n8847\t    - Do NOT use Read, Glob, or Grep tools on files already in project_context. SRDs, source code, ARCHITECTURE, VISION, shared_protocols, package_contracts — all are inline above. Reading them again wastes a turn and adds no information. Only use Read for files not present in project_context.\n8848\t    - Do NOT explore the filesystem. Do NOT run ls, find, tree, or similar commands.\n8849\t    - Do NOT modify files outside the Files to Create/Modify list unless a requirement explicitly demands it.\n8850\t    - Do NOT use bd or cupboard commands. Task tracking is handled externally.\n8851\t    - Do NOT invent interfaces, types, or patterns not described in the source code, Required Reading, or SRDs.\n8852\t    - Do NOT add features, refactoring, or improvements beyond what the requirements specify.\n8853\t    - Do NOT run any git commands. No git add, git commit, git init, git status, rm .git, or any other git operation. Git is managed externally by the orchestrator. Just write code and verify it compiles and passes tests.\n8854\t    - When building cmd/ binaries to verify compilation, use `go build -o bin/<name> ./cmd/<name>/` so outputs land in bin/ (which is git-ignored) rather than in the working directory.\n8855\t    - When a build or lint step fails, read ALL error and warning messages before making any changes. Fix every reported issue in a single edit pass, then run the build once to verify. Do not fix one issue, rebuild, fix the next — batch all fixes into one round.\n8856\t    - When modifying more than 3 locations in the same file, prefer the Write tool (full file replacement) over multiple Edit calls. Each Edit changes the file, invalidating old_string matches for subsequent Edits. Write avoids this by producing the complete file in one call.\n8857\t    - The test suite is the acceptance gate. Do not manually run binaries to compare output against reference implementations. Run `go test` and fix any failures. Manual comparison is only warranted when a test fails and the expected behavior is ambiguous.\n8858\t    - Complete all code changes before running `go build` or `go test`. Do not compile incrementally after each small edit. Mentally verify imports, types, and function signatures, then build once.\n8859\tdescription: |-\n8860\t    deliverable_type: code\n8861\t\n8862\t    required_reading:\n8863\t      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd109-dircolors.yaml (full SRD -- focus on R3.4 and R3.5)\n8864\t      - cmd/dircolors/main.go (existing implementation from prior tasks)\n8865\t      - cmd/dircolors/dircolors_test.go (existing tests from prior tasks)\n8866\t      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n8867\t\n8868\t    files:\n8869\t      - path: cmd/dircolors/main.go\n8870\t        action: modify\n8871\t        note: add R3.4 and R3.5 functionality\n8872\t      - path: cmd/dircolors/dircolors_test.go\n8873\t        action: modify\n8874\t        note: add differential tests for R3.4 and R3.5\n8875\t\n8876\t    requirements:\n8877\t      - id: R1\n8878\t        text: \"Implement srd109 R3.4 -- read the SRD for the exact requirement text and acceptance criteria\"\n8879\t      - id: R2\n8880\t        text: \"Implement srd109 R3.5 -- read the SRD for the exact requirement text and acceptance criteria\"\n8881\t\n8882\t    design_decisions:\n8883\t      - id: D1\n8884\t        text: \"Extend the existing main.go -- do not restructure or rewrite prior code\"\n8885\t      - id: D2\n8886\t        text: \"Add new DiffTest entries to the existing tests slice in dircolors_test.go\"\n8887\t      - id: D3\n8888\t        text: \"Reference binary is gdircolors via exec.LookPath; t.Skip if not found\"\n8889\t\n8890\t    acceptance_criteria:\n8891\t      - id: AC1\n8892\t        text: \"go build -o bin/dircolors ./cmd/dircolors/ compiles without errors\"\n8893\t      - id: AC2\n8894\t        text: \"go vet ./cmd/dircolors/ passes with no warnings\"\n8895\t      - id: AC3\n8896\t        text: \"go test ./cmd/dircolors/ -count=1 passes (all existing and new tests)\"\n8897\t      - id: AC4\n8898\t        text: \"SRD requirements R3.4 and R3.5 have corresponding differential test cases\"\n8899\t      - id: AC5\n8900\t        text: \"All 15 srd109 requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.5, R3.1-R3.5) are now implemented and tested across all four tasks\"\n8901\tshared_protocols:\n8902\t    - name: Differential testing\n8903\t      description: |\n8904\t        Every cmd/ package verifies its implementation by running the Go binary and the\n8905\t        Homebrew GNU reference binary side by side and comparing stdout, stderr, and exit\n8906\t        code via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests. Test cases are defined as []testutils.DiffTest\n8907\t        slices in <utility>_test.go files. The harness sets LC_ALL=C by default.\n8908\t      pattern: |\n8909\t        func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n8910\t            goBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n8911\t            refBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"g<utility>\")\n8912\t            if err != nil { t.Skip(\"reference binary not found\") }\n8913\t            tests := []testutils.DiffTest{ ... }\n8914\t            testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n8915\t        }\n8916\t    - name: Graceful skip on missing reference binary\n8917\t      description: |\n8918\t        All cmd/ test files call exec.LookPath for the Homebrew reference binary at the\n8919\t        start of TestDiff and call t.Skip if it is not found. This allows the test suite\n8920\t        to run on machines without Homebrew GNU binaries installed without reporting false\n8921\t        failures. The binary is never installed by the test itself.\n8922\t      pattern: |\n8923\t        refBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"g<utility>\")\n8924\t        if err != nil { t.Skipf(\"reference binary g<utility> not in PATH: %v\", err) }\n8925\t    - name: SIGPIPE handling\n8926\t      description: |\n8927\t        All cmd/ utilities that write to stdout call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at\n8928\t        the start of main. This ensures the process exits 0 when piped output is consumed\n8929\t        by a downstream consumer that closes its stdin (e.g., head, grep -q), matching GNU\n8930\t        coreutils behavior.\n8931\t      pattern: |\n8932\t        func main() {\n8933\t            sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n8934\t            ...\n8935\t        }\n8936\tpackage_contracts:\n8937\t    - srd_id: srd001-testutils\n8938\t      contract:\n8939\t        exports:\n8940\t            - name: DiffTest\n8941\t              signature: type DiffTest struct { Name string; Args []string; Stdin []byte; Env []string; WorkDir string; ExitCode int; Normalize []NormalizeFunc; ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte }\n8942\t            - name: NormalizeFunc\n8943\t              signature: type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n8944\t            - name: RunDiffTests\n8945\t              signature: func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest)\n8946\t            - name: ComposeNormalizers\n8947\t              signature: func ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc\n8948\t            - name: TimestampNormalizer\n8949\t              signature: var TimestampNormalizer NormalizeFunc\n8950\t        imports:\n8951\t            - testing\n8952\t            - os\n8953\t            - os/exec\n8954\t        import_constraints:\n8955\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n8956\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n8957\t    - srd_id: srd002-sys\n8958\t      contract:\n8959\t        exports:\n8960\t            - name: FileInfo\n8961\t              signature: type FileInfo struct { Mode os.FileMode; Size int64; Nlink uint64; Uid uint32; Gid uint32; Rdev uint64; Dev uint64; Ino uint64; Blocks int64; Blksize int64; ModTime time.Time; AccessTime time.Time; ChangeTime time.Time; Info os.FileInfo }\n8962\t            - name: Stat\n8963\t              signature: func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)\n8964\t            - name: Lstat\n8965\t              signature: func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)\n8966\t            - name: TerminalWidth\n8967\t              signature: func TerminalWidth() (int, error)\n8968\t            - name: IsTerminal\n8969\t              signature: func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool\n8970\t            - name: InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n8971\t              signature: func InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n8972\t            - name: OnTerminalResize\n8973\t              signature: func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int))\n8974\t        imports:\n8975\t            - os\n8976\t            - os/signal\n8977\t            - syscall\n8978\t            - time\n8979\t            - golang.org/x/sys/unix\n8980\t        import_constraints:\n8981\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n8982\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n8983\t    - srd_id: srd003-format\n8984\t      contract:\n8985\t        exports:\n8986\t            - name: HumanSizeOpts\n8987\t              signature: type HumanSizeOpts struct { Binary bool }\n8988\t            - name: HumanSize\n8989\t              signature: func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string\n8990\t            - name: FileTypeColor\n8991\t              signature: func FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string\n8992\t            - name: Reset\n8993\t              signature: func Reset() string\n8994\t            - name: ColorEnabled\n8995\t              signature: func ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool\n8996\t            - name: SetColorEnabled\n8997\t              signature: func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool)\n8998\t            - name: ResetColorEnabled\n8999\t              signature: func ResetColorEnabled()\n9000\t            - name: PadRight\n9001\t              signature: func PadRight(s string, width int) string\n9002\t            - name: PadLeft\n9003\t              signature: func PadLeft(s string, width int) string\n9004\t            - name: Columns\n9005\t              signature: func Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string\n9006\t        imports:\n9007\t            - io\n9008\t            - os\n9009\t            - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n9010\t        import_constraints:\n9011\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n9012\t    - srd_id: srd086-hashutil\n9013\t      contract:\n9014\t        exports:\n9015\t            - name: HashConfig\n9016\t              signature: type HashConfig struct { Algorithm string; NewHash func() hash.Hash; DigestLen int }\n9017\t            - name: CheckOptions\n9018\t              signature: type CheckOptions struct { Warn bool; Quiet bool; Status bool }\n9019\t            - name: FormatGNU\n9020\t              signature: func FormatGNU(digest, filename string, binary bool) string\n9021\t            - name: FormatBSDTag\n9022\t              signature: func FormatBSDTag(algorithm, filename, digest string) string\n9023\t            - name: ComputeDigest\n9024\t              signature: func ComputeDigest(r io.Reader, cfg HashConfig) (string, error)\n9025\t            - name: ParseChecksumLine\n9026\t              signature: func ParseChecksumLine(line string, cfg HashConfig) (filename, expectedDigest string, binary bool, err error)\n9027\t            - name: VerifyChecksums\n9028\t              signature: func VerifyChecksums(checksumFile string, cfg HashConfig, opts CheckOptions, stdout, stderr io.Writer) (bool, error)\n9029\t            - name: DigestFiles\n9030\t              signature: func DigestFiles(files []string, cfg HashConfig, binary, tag bool, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n9031\t        imports:\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Aa28RpuWwgp6wcrhbpJVuq","session_id":"c1ab43ae-f5c9-4855-a043-505b10e6a94f","uuid":"f4b5a7b5-9801-4167-a54d-9a7fc92d3e80","timestamp":"2026-05-28T16:07:14.861Z"}
